 ## Overview
-Mesh SDF Sampling creates a signed distance sampler function from a `THREE.BufferGeometry` and a world transform matrix, returning a function that computes the shortest signed distance to the mesh at any given world-space coordinate [S1, unofficial]. Negative values indicate the point is inside the mesh, and positive values indicate it is outside [S1, unofficial].
+Mesh SDF Sampling constructs a callable signed distance field (SDF) sampler function from a `THREE.BufferGeometry` instance and a world transform matrix [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently evaluating the shortest signed distance from arbitrary world-space coordinates to a triangle mesh surface, where negative values indicate interior points and positive values indicate exterior points [S1, unofficial]. It differs from generic geometry samplers by specifically handling the `THREE.Mesh` abstraction, including automatic world matrix updates, and delegates the core geometric computation to a lower-level geometry sampler [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Inputs:** `geometry` (a `THREE.BufferGeometry` instance containing position attributes and an index array) and `transformMatrix` (a `THREE.Matrix4` representing the world transform) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Output:** A function `sampler(x, y, z)` returning a `number`: the signed distance [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs:**
+- `geometry`: A `THREE.BufferGeometry` instance. Must be indexed.
+- `matrix`: A `THREE.Matrix4` representing the world transform of the mesh.
+
+**Outputs:**
+- A function `sampler(x, y, z)` returning a `number` (the signed distance).
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- `worldPositions`: An array of `Vector3` objects, precomputed from the geometry's position attributes transformed by `transformMatrix`. Size: `geometry.attributes.position.count` [S1, unofficial].
-- Temporary vectors (allocated once to avoid per-query allocations): `va`, `vb`, `vc` (triangle vertices), `ab`, `ac` (edge vectors), `p` (query point), `q` (closest point on triangle), `bestPoint` (closest point found so far), `bestNormal` (normal at `bestPoint`), `toPoint` (vector from `bestPoint` to `p`) [S1, unofficial].
-
